      "id": "s1",
      "nodeId": "n3",
      "text": "Despite the seemingly ubiquitous admonition to \"drink at least eight 8-oz glasses of water a day\" (with an accompanying reminder that beverages containing caffeine and alcohol do not count), rigorous proof for this counsel appears to be lacking.",
      "contextBefore": "f physiology. Regulatory, integrative and comparative physiology · 2002-11-01 · 283(5):R993-1004 · DOI: 10.1152/ajpregu.00365.2002 · PMID: 12376390 Publication type: Journal Article, Review Abstract. ",
      "contextAfter": " This review sought to find the origin of this advice (called \"8 x 8\" for short) and to examine the scientific evidence, if any, that might support it. The search included not only electronic modes bu",
      "start": 328,
      "end": 573,
      "extractSha256": "4d0af0069110045105e9b875c53275ac1023640b4a57dca2ee0beff160885159",
      "windowSha256": "a86f16e1b300de945510dbd4108fd65699cd2587d465c2b2e9b6ecd3754f89c0",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-17T06:06:07.941Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20200307152749/https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12376390",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "nodeId": "n4",
      "text": "A survey of 3,003 Americans shows that: \\* Americans consume 12.8 8-ounce servings of beverages each day, but only 4.6 are water. \\* 65% of Americans know that eight 8-ounce glasses of water is the recommended daily serving. \\* However, only 21% drink eight or more glasses a day. \\* 35% drink three or fewer servings a day. \\* 9% drink no water at all. \\* St.",
      "contextBefore": " drink it, you'd have better luck with a horse. And don't even get us started on the fact that people need even more water during exercise, roughly an extra 4 ounces for every 15 minutes of activity. ",
      "contextAfter": " Louis residents drink the least water of any of the participating cities in the survey. \\* Several cities, including Los Angeles, Miami, Tampa and St. Louis, tend to avoid their tap water more than t",
      "start": 329,
      "end": 689,
      "extractSha256": "50a069c3f16012b7da2a14033b2789aefc33f208bd53f1197dadee61d8883891",
      "windowSha256": "d98feb31c74c569b06adbdfb3ae4ce3b8983d75f2113de4bd57421c12603321e",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-17T06:06:32.487Z",
      "waybackUrl": null,
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "nodeId": "n5",
      "text": "But with Hong Kong knockoffs of $35 designer glasses available at $8 a pair, sunglasses have been so democratized that the first meaning hardly applies.",
      "contextBefore": "ion sunglasses lies in their unique ability to make two visual statements simultaneously: \"I want to be alone\" and \"Notice me.\" Traditionally, these are attitudes ascribed to the rich and celebrated. ",
      "contextAfter": " In 1979, sunglasses are about being noticed. Until very recently, almost all efforts to use eyeglasses as attention-getters were colorrelated. In 1971, the United States Army announced that regulatio",
